          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            are defined as in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
            of 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+theories"} worldline theories
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"}> Feynman diagrams
            ) replaced by those of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFTs"} 2d CFTs
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/StringFeynmanDiagrams.png", :width => "300"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Road_to_Reality"} Penrose 04
              %a{:href => "https://benjaminjurke.com/content/articles/2010/string-theory/"} Jurke 10
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#Finiteness Finiteness
          %p
            The amplitudes are thought (see the commented
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#References"} references below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
            and number of punctures of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Riemann+surface"}> super
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surfaces"}> Riemann surfaces
            ) finite (at least UV-finite), hence
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalized
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            of these effective field theories (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            coupled to
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The full
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum"} sum
              of all these (finite) contributions over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genera
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surfaces"} Riemann surfaces
              (the “loop orders”). This
              %em sum
              diverges, even if all loop orders are finite. Notice though, that a non-trivial
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
              is not supposed to have a finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius+of+convergence"} radius of convergence
              of its scattering amplitudes, since that would imply convergence also for
              %em negative
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+string"} bosonic string
              the perturbation series has apparently been explicitly shown not to be
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+summation"} Borel resummable
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative effect
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "string+theory+FAQ#NonConvergenceOfPerturbationSeries"} string theory FAQ – Is the divergence of the pertubation series fatal?
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              A string scattering amplitude is called
              %em UV-finite
              at a given loop order (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              marked points/string insertions) if the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlation+function"} correlation function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \phi_1, \cdots, \phi_n\rangle_{\Sigma}
              is finite for every single such
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g,n)
              though is the averaging of this over all possible
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+structures"} conformal structures
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
              of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlation+function"} correlation function
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+conformal+structures"} moduli space of conformal structures
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ,
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}_{g,n}
              (a
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stack"} Deligne-Mumford stack
            %p
              If also this integral is finite, hence if the total
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+conformal+structures"} moduli space of conformal structures
              is finite, then one says the amplitude is
              = succeed "." do
                %em IR-finite
            %p
              This distinction between UV-finiteness and IR-finiteness is not always highlighted in all of the articles below. All authors argue that the string is
              %em UV-finite
              to all order. The IR-finiteness is only discussed much more recently at low loop order.
            %p
              IR non-finiteness is not physically fatal. For instance if a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbative
              theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
              develops a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
              perturbatively, then the perturbation series will be IR-divergent, signifying the fact that background spacetime without cosmological constant is no longer a solution to the quantum-corrected
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
              perturbation series. For the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
              case this can already be seen from the fact that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFT"} effective QFT
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
              etc. does not admit a cosmological constant, for that would violate
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
          %h3#openclosed_scattering_duality_and_klt_relations Open-closed scattering duality and KLT relations
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open%2Fclosed+string+duality"} open/closed string duality
            implies certain relations in string scattering amplitudes that in the point-particle limit induces relations between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            and in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KLT+relations"} KLT relations
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#MafraSchlotterer18a"} Mafra-Schlotterer 18a
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#MafraSchlotterer18b"} Mafra-Schlotterer 18b
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#MafraSchlotterer18c"} Mafra-Schlotterer 18c
          %h3#twistor_string_amplitudes_and_mhv_amplitudes_in_yangmills_theory Twistor string amplitudes and MHV amplitudes in Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            The scattering amplitudes in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twistor+string+theory"} twistor string theory
            induce the MHV amplitudes in (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"}> super
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"}> Yang-Mills theory
            \. See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+results+applied+elsewhere"} string theory results applied elsewhere
            in the section
